I verified end-to-end reachability and routing for the 172.16.30.0/24 (Campus 1 VLAN30) from Campus 2 (10.5.0.0/24). The Campus 1 Layer-3 gateway for VLAN30 is on mswitch0 (SVI vlan30 = 172.16.30.254/24), and it is actively advertising 172.16.30.0/24 into OSPF (mswitch0 FRR OSPF includes networks 172.16.10/24, 172.16.20/24, 172.16.30/24). All transit routers (router7, router10, router11, router16, router17, router8) have OSPF routes to 172.16.30.0/24 installed and pointing toward the correct next hops. A targeted reachability sweep shows pc1 (10.5.0.1) successfully pinging pc12 (172.16.30.12) with no loss (avg RTT ~10.6 ms), and direct pings from multiple core routers to 172.16.30.12 also succeed. On the host side, a 172.16.30 machine (pc12) has the correct default gateway 172.16.30.254.

Mechanistically, nothing in the routing domain is filtering or missing the 172.16.30.0/24; OSPF adjacencies are stable and prefixes are present. Localization indicates the Campus 1 VLAN30 gateway and inter-campus paths are functioning as designed.

Root cause: No network fault currently present; the reported symptom is not reproducible. 

Corrective fix: No corrective action is required on the network. If the user still experiences issues from 10.5.0.1, check the PC’s local firewall/route cache (flush ARP: ip neigh flush all) and confirm its default gateway. From the network perspective, 172.16.30.0/24 is reachable and correctly routed. 
